\setlength{\footskip}{8mm}

\chapter{CONCLUSION}

This thesis set out from a simple observation about human communication: in therapy, what a client says and how they say it do not always agree. Text-only AI counselors are blind to this gap. They read the words ``I am fine'' and have no way to hear the tremor underneath them. We proposed that emotional dissonance, computed as the Valence-Arousal mismatch between a client's text and their voice, can serve as an explicit signal that guides a CBT-oriented LLM therapist toward deeper, more empathetic, and more clinically appropriate responses.

We implemented this idea as a complete multimodal pipeline. Client utterances are analyzed in two parallel channels: vad-bert extracts text-based Valence-Arousal values, and WavLM extracts speech-based Valence-Arousal values from the vocal delivery, with librosa providing natural-language vocal descriptors. The per-dimension delta between the two channels, flagged when either exceeds a fixed threshold of 0.5, is injected directly into the therapist's prompt as a dissonance signal, instructing the model to gently explore what might lie beneath the surface when words and voice diverge.

Our experiments evaluated four therapist conditions across four LLM backbones on 100 shared-problem-seed dialogues each (1,600 sessions), using a three-layer blind protocol: absolute GPT-4o grading on seven MIRROR/CTRS-derived metrics, per-seed paired statistics, and 800 swap-consistent pairwise preference comparisons with a cross-family second judge. For RQ1, the Dissonance-Aware condition achieved the highest score on all seven metrics on GPT-4o mini, with significant gains over Baseline on the CTRS craft dimensions (Guided Discovery, Focus, Strategy; paired $t = 3.1$--$4.7$; pairwise net win-rate $0.680$, $p = 0.0001$), and a significant advantage on DeepSeek V3.2 that only the pairwise layer could detect (net $0.660$, $p = 0.00004$) because the absolute scale saturates for that backbone. The study's sharpest mechanistic finding came from the comparison against Multimodal (Vocal-Aware): with identical vocal inputs but without the dissonance computation and framing, therapist quality \emph{regressed below the text-only baseline} on both weaker backbones (GPT-4o mini $t = -4.75$; Qwen $t = -6.13$), while Dissonance-Aware defeated it decisively (GPT-4o mini: Guided Discovery $+0.75$, $t = 10.1$; pairwise net win-rate $0.800$, $p < 10^{-4}$). Raw vocal metadata is therefore not intrinsically therapeutic; the dissonance reasoning layer is what converts vocal signals into clinical value. For RQ2, the advantage proved headroom-gated rather than universal: significant on GPT-4o mini and DeepSeek, neutral on Qwen (reported as-is, corroborated by both judges), and unmeasurable at Claude's ceiling --- but the ordering against the other information conditions held on every backbone, with no significant loss in the pairwise layer anywhere.

The contributions of this work are fourfold. First, we introduced a multimodal framework that aligns independently extracted text and speech emotional representations for therapist response generation. Second, we operationalized emotional dissonance as a prompt-level therapeutic signal, reframing what prior work treated as multimodal failure into a clinically useful cue of hidden affect --- and demonstrated empirically that this reframing, not the vocal data itself, produces the gain. Third, we established a controlled four-condition, four-backbone experimental design, paired on shared problem seeds with stateful simulated clients, that isolates the contribution of each emotional metadata type. Fourth, we delivered a reproducible three-layer blind evaluation protocol --- absolute grading, paired statistics, and swap-consistent pairwise preference with cross-judge reliability measurement --- that remains discriminative when absolute LLM-judge scores saturate, together with a documented account of when and why each layer is necessary.

The broader implication is encouraging for accessible mental health technology: emotionally perceptive therapeutic AI does not require frontier-scale models or expensive training. A modest model, given an explicit text-voice dissonance signal and the instruction to explore it gently, approaches the unaided performance of a model many times its price (Understanding 4.98 vs.\ 5.13 for Claude's baseline). Equally important is the negative half of the finding: handing the same model raw vocal features without that framing makes it worse. For real deployments where cost and privacy constrain model choice, the practical path is not more signals but better-structured signals.

We close with candor about what remains. Our evidence comes from synthetic, English-language dialogues judged by LLMs whose cross-family agreement is only fair at item level ($\kappa = 0.217$) though directionally consistent (no opposing lean in any of the six cells with first-judge signal); our TTS client voices are produced by an alpha-stage engine whose prosody rarely generates genuine mismatch; and on two backbones the instruments could not resolve differences at all. The immediate next steps are clear: human expert validation of the existing pairwise comparisons, cross-turn dissonance tracking with an adaptive threshold, controlled mismatch manipulation, and replay with real human voices. We regard the present results as a foundation: emotional dissonance is measurable, injectable, and --- where it can be measured --- demonstrably useful, and the door is now open to richer, more human-like multimodal therapeutic reasoning.
